 ],
 "title": "The Science of Functional Programming: A Tutorial, with Examples in Scala",
 "byline": "Sergei Winitzki · version of 4 September 2024, lulu.com (GNU FDL)",
 "note": "At about 1,300 pages, summaries and terms are given per chapter; each numbered section is still listed with its PDF page range. Exercise, summary and solved-example subsections and appendices other than C are not broken out. There is no HTML edition. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "A mathematically rigorous course in functional programming that derives FP idioms and their laws by symbolic reasoning, with Scala code. The introductory part treats functions as formulas, induction and recursion, disjunctive types, curried and fully parametric functions, and the Curry–Howard correspondence with type isomorphisms. The intermediate part develops functors and contrafunctors, a notation for deriving code, typeclasses and their structural derivation, filterable functors, monads, applicatives and traversables, proving laws and building instances systematically. The advanced part covers free typeclass constructions (free monads, applicatives) and an extensive theory of monad transformers. Essays argue for “applied functional type theory”, and an appendix proves parametricity and naturality laws."
}